In [[particle physics]] a '''Majorana fermion''' ({{IPAc-en|m|aɪ|ə|'|r|ɑː|n|ə}}<ref name=youtube_pronunciation>{{YouTube| XHUgetdkJmg | "Quantum Computation possible with Majorana Fermions" }}, uploaded 19 April 2013, retrieved 5 October 2014; and also based on [[Ettore Majorana|the pronunciation of physicist's name]].</ref>) or '''Majorana particle''' is a [[fermion]] that is its own [[antiparticle]]. They were hypothesised by [[Ettore Majorana]] in 1937. The term is sometimes used in opposition to [[Dirac fermion]], which describes fermions that are not their own antiparticles.

With the exception of [[neutrino]]s, all of the [[Standard Model]] elementary fermions are known to behave as Dirac fermions at low energy (lower than the [[electroweak symmetry breaking]] temperature), and none are Majorana fermions. The nature of neutrinos is not settled – they may be either Dirac or Majorana fermions.

In [[condensed matter physics]], [[quasiparticle]] [[Excited state|excitations]] can appear like bound Majorana states. However, instead of a single [[fundamental particle]], they are the collective movement of several individual particles (themselves composite) which are governed by [[Anyon#Non-abelian anyons|non-Abelian statistics]].

== Theory ==
The concept goes back to Majorana's suggestion in 1937<ref>{{cite book |last1=Majorana |first1=Ettore |last2=Maiani |first2=Luciano |year=2006 |chapter=A symmetric theory of electrons and positrons |editor-first=Giuseppe Franco |editor-last=Bassani |title=Ettore Majorana Scientific Papers |isbn=978-3-540-48091-4 |doi=10.1007/978-3-540-48095-2_10 |pages=[https://archive.org/details/ettoremajoranasc00bass/page/n230 201]–233 |s2cid=17529013 |url=https://archive.org/details/ettoremajoranasc00bass |url-access=limited}} Translated from: {{cite journal |last=Majorana |first=Ettore |year=1937 |title=Teoria simmetrica dell'elettrone e del positrone |journal=Il Nuovo Cimento |volume=14 |issue=4 |pages=171–184 |language=it |bibcode=1937NCim...14..171M |s2cid=18973190 |doi=10.1007/bf02961314}}</ref> that electrically neutral [[Spin (physics)|spin]]-<math>\tfrac{1}{2}</math> particles can be described by a [[real number|real-valued]] [[relativistic wave equations|wave equation]] (the [[Majorana equation]]), and would therefore be identical to their antiparticle, because the wave functions of particle and antiparticle are related by [[complex conjugate|complex conjugation]], which leaves the Majorana wave equation unchanged.

The difference between Majorana fermions and Dirac fermions can be expressed mathematically in terms of the [[creation and annihilation operators]] of [[second quantization]]: The creation operator <math>\gamma^{\dagger}_j</math> creates a fermion in quantum state <math>j</math> (described by a ''real'' wave function), whereas the annihilation operator <math>\gamma_j</math> annihilates it (or, equivalently, creates the corresponding antiparticle). For a Dirac fermion the operators <math>\gamma^{\dagger}_j</math> and <math>\gamma_j</math> are distinct, whereas for a Majorana fermion they are identical. The ordinary fermionic annihilation and creation operators <math>f</math> and <math>f^{\dagger}</math> can be written in terms of two Majorana operators <math>\gamma_1</math> and <math>\gamma_2</math> by
:<math> f =  \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} (\gamma_1+i\gamma_2),</math>
:<math> f^{\dagger} =  \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} (\gamma_1-i\gamma_2) ~.</math>
which can be rearranged to obtain the Majorana fermion operators as
:<math> \gamma_1 =  \frac{f^\dagger + f}{\sqrt{2}},  </math>
:<math> \gamma_2 = i \frac{f^\dagger - f}{\sqrt{2}}. </math>
With this normalization, the Majorana operators satisfy the ordinary fermionic anticommutation relations <math>\{\gamma_i, \gamma_j\} = \delta_{ij}</math>. They then have a norm of <math>\gamma_i^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}</math> making them <math>\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}</math> times the generators of the real [[Clifford algebra]]. This is the convention used in the literature on the [[Sachdev–Ye–Kitaev model]] (SYK model), where it is often fixed implicitly through the free [[Euclidean space|Euclidean]] propagator <math>G(\tau) = \langle T \gamma_i(\tau) \gamma_i(0) \rangle = \tfrac{1}{2} \operatorname{sgn}(\tau)</math>.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Maldacena |first1=Juan |last2=Stanford |first2=Douglas |year=2016 |title=Remarks on the Sachdev-Ye-Kitaev model |journal=Physical Review D |volume=94 |issue=10  |arxiv=1604.07818 |doi=10.1103/PhysRevD.94.106002}}</ref>


In [[supersymmetry]] models, [[neutralino]]s – [[superpartner]]s of [[gauge boson]]s and [[Higgs boson]]s – are Majorana fermions.

=== Identities ===
Another common convention for the normalization of the Majorana fermion [[creation and annihilation operators|operator]] <math>\gamma</math> is
:<math> f = \tfrac{1}{2} (\gamma_1 + i\gamma_2), </math>
:<math> f^\dagger = \tfrac{1}{2} (\gamma_1 - i\gamma_2), </math>
which can be rearranged to obtain the Majorana fermion operators as
:<math> \gamma_1 =  f^\dagger + f,  </math>
:<math> \gamma_2 = i (f^\dagger - f). </math>
It is easy to see that <math>\gamma_i=\gamma_i^\dagger</math> is indeed fulfilled. This convention has the advantage that the Majorana operator [[Involution (mathematics)|squares to the identity]], i.e. <math>\gamma_i^2=(\gamma_i^\dagger)^2=1</math>. Using this convention, a collection of <math>2n</math> Majorana fermions (<math>n</math> ordinary fermions), <math>\gamma_i</math> (<math>i=1,2,..,2n</math>) obey the following [[Anticommutator|anticommutation]] identities
: <math> \{\gamma_i,\gamma_j\} = 2 \delta_{ij} </math>
and
: <math>\sum_{ijkl} [\gamma_i A_{ij} \gamma_j, \gamma_k B_{kl} \gamma_l] = 4 \sum_{ij} \gamma_i [A,B]_{ij} \gamma_j </math>
where <math>A</math> and <math>B</math> are [[skew-symmetric matrix|antisymmetric]] matrices. These are identical to the commutation relations for the real [[Clifford algebra]] in <math>n</math> dimensions (<math>\mathrm{Cl}( \mathbb {R}^n )</math>).

== Elementary particles ==
Because particles and antiparticles have opposite conserved charges, Majorana fermions have zero charge, hence among the fundamental particles, the only fermions that could be Majorana are massive neutrinos. All the other elementary fermions of the [[Standard Model]] have [[Charge (physics)|gauge charge]]s, so they cannot have fundamental [[Majorana mass]]es: Even the Standard Model's left-handed neutrinos and right-handed antineutrinos have non-zero [[weak isospin]], <math>T_3 = \pm \tfrac{1}{2} ,</math> a [[Charge (physics)|charge-like]] quantum number. However, if they exist, the so-called "Majorana neutrinos" would be [[truly neutral particle]]s (assuming no other, unknown gauge charges exist).

[[File:Ettore Majorana.jpg|thumb|right|upright|[[Ettore Majorana]] hypothesised the existence of Majorana fermions in 1937]]
The  massive neutrino was introduced to explain [[neutrino oscillation]] but fails to fully explain the anomalously small Standard Model [[neutrino mass]]es. This leads many to speculate that neutrinos could have Majorana masses. If they do, then at low energy (after [[electroweak symmetry breaking]]), by the [[seesaw mechanism]], the neutrino fields would naturally behave as six Majorana fields, with three of them expected to have very high masses (comparable to the [[GUT scale]]) and the other three expected to have very low masses (below 1&nbsp;eV). If right-handed neutrinos exist but do not have a Majorana mass, the neutrinos would instead behave as three [[Dirac fermion]]s and their antiparticles with masses coming directly from the [[Higgs interaction]], like the other Standard Model fermions.

The seesaw mechanism is appealing because it would naturally explain why the observed neutrino masses are so small. However, if the neutrinos are Majorana then they violate the conservation of [[lepton number]] and even of [[B − L]].

